Lay-What: A failover drill test no longer fails at random on a busy machine.
Lay-How: The test now stops its pretend caller in a way that cannot be ignored, and waits for all its helpers to finish before it checks.
Lay-Why: Its random failures blocked unrelated work from being saved; nothing a user sees changes.
Tech-What: spl-role-failover-drill.tst.sh case 9 RED CONTROL is deterministic; the orc drill function is unchanged.
Tech-How: the kill stub ends the caller's group with KILL, not HUP; the control waits on wait_event env_idle SPOOL_ROOT before asserting no failover.
Tech-Why: runs started under nohup inherited SIGHUP ignored, so the group survived the HUP stub and the non-detached drill ran the failover.
